                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                also have equal cardinality (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Kleppmann14"} Kleppmann14
            %li
              %p
                That every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
          %h2#Variants Variants
          %p
            There are a number of weaker axioms which are implied by the full axiom of choice. Some of these are valid or accepted more generally than the full AC, and/or suffice for some of the usual applications of choice. In particular, the full axiom of choice is generally rejected in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Many applications of choice in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                require only the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+principle"} ultrafilter principle
                (UF), or equivalently the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Boolean prime ideal theorem
            %li
              %p
                From the perspective of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                (EM) may be seen as a weak form of the axiom of choice; EM is equivalent to the statement that every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuratowski-finite+set"} Kuratowski-finite set
                is projective. See at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu-Goodman-Myhill+theorem"} Diaconescu-Goodman-Myhill theorem
            %li
              %p
                A very weak form of choice (which follows from EM) is the statement that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supports+split"} supports split
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                The axioms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
                (CC) and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
                (DC) suffice for many of the usual applications of choice in the analysis of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural numbers
                is projective. DC strenghtens CC by allowing the set of possible choices for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
                to depend on the choice made for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                The axiom
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolution"} projective resolutions
                and cofibrant replacements, as well as the usual theorems in algebra that (for example)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
                has enough projectives. For example, see the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure+on+Cat"} canonical model structure on Cat
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+small+violations+of+choice"} axiom of small violations of choice
                (SVC) asserts there is a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                such that every set is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ×
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\times S
                for some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+object"} choice set
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolutions
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+multiple+choice"} axiom of multiple choice
                is a different way of saying that choice is violated in only a small way, which is more “local” than SVC. It apparently follows from SVC, at least in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+cardinality+selection+axiom"} small cardinality selection axiom
                is another similar axiom. It asserts that there is a class function selecting for every set an isomorphic set (its “cardinality”) such that among each isomorphism class of sets, the collection of all “cardinalities” forms only a set.
            %li
              %p
                A still weaker axiom along the lines of “AC fails in only a small way,” which is implied by AMC, is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WISC"} WISC
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/X
                consisting of the surjections has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+initial+set"} weakly initial set
                (under COSHEP it has a single weakly initial object, namely a projective cover of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+exact+completion"} free exact completion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mi ex
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi lex
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{ex/lex}
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                (i.e. that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                has a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generic+proof"} generic proof
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mi ex
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi lex
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{ex/lex}
                is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} well-powered
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dominance"} dominance
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \subseteq \Omega
                and restricting choice to the
                = succeed "-open" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R:A \times B \to \Sigma
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                contains a functional entire relation, i.e. there exists a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \to B
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x, f(x)) = \top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+domain"} boolean domain
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+choice"} decidable choice
          %p The axiom of choice can also be strengthened in a few ways.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                While the ordinary axiom of choice says that any surjection of sets is split, the
                %em axiom of global choice
                says that this is also true for any surjection of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            %li
              %p
                One can also postulate a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+operator"} choice operator
                %em specified
                way to choose an element from any nonempty set. This implies global choice, and conversely a choice operator can be defined from any well-ordering of the class of all sets.
          %p
            Finally, one can instead adopt the
            %em negation
            of the axiom of choice, or a strengthened version of this negation:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The assumption that every subset of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                has the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+property"} Baire property
                (BP) is consistent with DC but not AC; the same holds for the assumption that every subset of the real line is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} measurable
                (LM) if at least one
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
                exists. These assumptions leads to a very nice setting for analysis called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dream+mathematics"} dream mathematics
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+determinacy"} axiom of determinacy
                is a natural statement in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/game+theory"} game theory
                that is consistent with DC; in fact, it implies DC in certain models, such as in the constructible (in the sense of Goedel) closure of the set of reals. However, determinacy contradicts full AC (for example, it implies LM and BP, as in the previous entry).
            %li
              %p
                Any of the varieties of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
                that contradict excluded middle necessarily contradict choice, but they are usually (if not always) consistent with DC (and even COSHEP).
            %li
              %p
                The existence of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding#Inconsistency"} Reinhardt cardinal
                contradicts AC.
          %h3#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %p
            To formulate a version of the axiom of choice in a higher category, one has to make an appropriate choice of the meaning of “epimorphism”. In most cases, it is best to choose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphisms"} effective epimorphism in an (infinity,1)-category
            or a related notion such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eso+morphisms"} eso morphisms
          %p
            Less obviously, we usually want to also impose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object"} truncation
            requirements on at least some of the objects involved in the axiom of choice. It seems usually necessary to require the codomain to be 0-truncated (axioms of choice without this requirement tend to be inconsistent); as for the domain we can choose to or not.
          %ul
            %li
              An
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  axiom of
                  = succeed "-choice" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AC
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_n
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %span.newWikiWord
                truncated morphism
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/truncated+morphism"} ?
              with 0-truncated codomain has a section. We write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AC
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_\infty
              for the
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong axiom of infinity-choice
              %em every
              morphism with 0-truncated codomain has a section.
          %p
            These are stronger axioms as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            increases. The “difference” between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AC
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AC
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_\infty
            is roughly the axiom that
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets+cover"} sets cover
          %p
            For
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,k)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\gt 1
            it is unclear whether it is sensible to allow the domain to be non-groupoidal.
          %ul
            %li
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/michaelshulman/show/axiom+of+2-choice"} axiom of 2-choice
              has been proposed to mean that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eso+morphism"} eso morphism
              with groupoidal domain and 0-truncated codomain has a section.
          %p There are also “internal” versions of these axioms.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                (the internal logic of an
                = succeed "-topos)," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AC
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_n
                is “every surjection onto a set with
                = succeed "-type" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo :
                          %mi Set
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo :
                          %mi X
                          %mo →
                          %mi n
                          %mi Type
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ‖
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ‖
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{(X:Set)} \prod_{(Y:X\to n Type)} \Big( \prod_{(x:X)} \Vert Y(x)\Vert \to \Big\Vert \prod_{(x:X)} Y(x) \Big\Vert \Big)
            %li
              %p
                More generally, we can replace the
                = succeed "-truncation" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
                = succeed "-truncation" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi AC
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_{k,n}
            %li
              %p
                We can also replace the
                = succeed "-truncation" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
                = succeed "-connectedness," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    axiom of
                    = succeed "-connected" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %h3#InTypeTheory In type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            in the type theory;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+some+types"} propositions as some types
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+axiom+of+choice"} propositional axiom of choice
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theoretic+axiom+of+choice"} type theoretic axiom of choice
          %p
            The former corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+axiom+of+choice"} internal axiom of choice
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            which interprets such categories.
          %h3#in_spatial_type_theory In spatial type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+type+theory"} spatial type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            = succeed "," do
              %strong sharp axiom of choice
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msubsup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ac
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                        %mo ♯
                      %mo :
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo :
                            %mi A
                        %mo ♯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo )
                      %mo →
                      %mo ♯
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo .
                      %mo ♯
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma, x:A \vdash B(x) \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{ac}^{\sharp}_{A, B}:\left(\prod_{x:A} \sharp [B(x)]\right) \to \sharp\forall x:A.\sharp B(x)}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp A
            is the sharp modality of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x:A.B(x)
            is the propositional truncation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:A} B(x)
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %ul
            %li
              The axiom of choice implies the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
              (due to Diaconesu 1975) see
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#McLarty"} McLarty, theorem 17.9
              %em
                %a{:href => "excluded+middle#RelationToTheAxiomOfChoice"} excluded middle – Relation to axiom of choice
          %p
            See also the
            %a{:href => "#ConsequencesDatabase"} choice consequences data base
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+axiom+of+choice"} propositional axiom of choice
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering"} well-ordering
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+choice"} finite choice
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HAF"} Eric Schechter's analysis book
              surveys several variants of AC and its negation with a view to applications of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ac-chart-7dd95d8c-7395-40f1-8c6d-9d3cbe59d957.gif"}/
          %p (Here, UF, DC, CC, BP, and LM are as defined above.)
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            is in
          %ul
            %li#McLarty
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                , section 17.6 of
                %em Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes
            %li#ConsequencesDatabase
              %p
                The
                %a{:href => "http://consequences.emich.edu/conseq.htm"} Consequences of the Axiom of Choice Project
                provides an interactive data base that can be used to search for implications between various (weakened) forms of the Axiom of Choice.
                %a{:href => "http://cgraph.inters.co/"} Choiceless grapher
                (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/ioannad/jeffrey"} github.com/ioannad/jeffrey
            %li
              %p
                Gonçalo Gutierres da Conceição,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Axiom of Countable Choice in Topology
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~ggutc/teses/teseingles.pdf"} pdf
              %p Despite the title, this covers more than countable choice, but the focus is on sequential aspects (metric spaces, first- and second-countable spaces, etc).
          %p A classical reference for AC in toposes is section 5.2 (pp.140ff) in
          %ul
            %li#JT77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em Topos Theory
              , Academic Press New York 1977. (Reprinted by Dover Mineola 2014)
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Blass"}> Andreas Blass
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology detects failures of the axiom of choice
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/tran/1983-279-01/S0002-9947-1983-0704615-7/"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Cohomology
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                blog
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2013/07/24/cohomology/"} here
          %p
            and to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groups"} free groups
            in
          %ul
            %li#Kleppmann14
              Philipp Kleppmann,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Generating sets of free groups and the axiom of choice
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/malq.201300088"} doi
          %p An alternative form of AC in toposes using injectivity is explored in
          %ul
            %li
              Toby Kenney,
              %em Injective Power Objects and the Axiom of Choice
              , JPAA
              %strong 215
              (2011) pp.131–144.
          %p The result on IAC in toposes stems from
          %ul
            %li#FreydScedrov90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> P. J. Freyd
              , A. Scedrov,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories%2C+Allegories"} Categories, Allegories
              , North-Holland Amsterdam 1990.
          %h3#ReferencesInHomotopy In homotopy type theory
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#HoTTBook
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
                , section 3.8 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"}> Egbert Rijke
                , section 17.4 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.11082"} arXiv:2212.11082
          %h3#in_spatial_type_theory_2 In spatial type theory
          %p For the sharp axiom of choice in spatial type theory, see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Brouwer’s fixed-point theorem in real-cohesive homotopy type theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07584"} arXiv:1509.07584
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129517000147"} doi:10.1017/S0960129517000147
          .property
            category:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/foundational+axiom"} foundational axiom
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/logic"} logic
